#{[17592186531882 :question/problem "What is the range of the function $$\n\\displaystyle y=- \\frac{9}{2}\\sin(3x+\\pi)-\\frac{10}{3}\n$$?"] [17592186531895 :question/problem "What is the range of the function $$\n\\displaystyle y=- \\frac{5}{2}\\cos(x-15^{\\circ})-\\frac{14}{3}\n$$?"] [17592186531909 :question/problem "What is the range of the function $$\n\\displaystyle y=- \\frac{4}{3}\\sin(4x+\\frac{\\pi}{6})+\\frac{14}{3}\n$$?"] [17592186531915 :question/problem "What is the minimum value of $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=- \\frac{5}{2}\\cos(5x-2)-\\frac{11}{2}\n$$?"] [17592186532621 :question/problem "Starting with the standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x),$ find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion for $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186532622 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{4}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186532622 :question/worked-solution "Writing $$\n\\displaystyle b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={b}^{T}x.\n$$\n\nThe gradient of a function $f(x)={b}^{T}x$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=b.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186532629 :question/problem "Using the standard form of the Maclaurin series of $\\frac{1}{1+x},$ find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion of the definite integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186532632 :question/problem "Using the standard form of the Maclaurin series of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion of the definite integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{x})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186532638 :question/problem "Starting with the standard Maclaurin expansion for ${e}^{x},$ find the first four non-zero terms of the series expansion for $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{e}^{x}-1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186532640 :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle S=\\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{3}{14}+\\frac{4}{21}+⋯+\\frac{21}{140}+\\frac{22}{147}\n$$ in sigma notation.\n\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,\\,\\,\\,S=$ | $\\sum $ | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  | $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{}^{n=1}$ |  |  | $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$ |"] [17592186532643 :question/problem "Given that $u=x+3$, which integral is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x\\sqrt[4]{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [17592186532645 :question/problem "Find the sum $$\n\\displaystyle \\sqrt[3]{\\pi }-1-\\frac{(\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }-1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }-1)^{3}}{3}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }-1)^{n}}{n}+⋯.\n$$"] [17592186532646 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=(Ax-2b)^{T}(Ax-2b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [17592186532646 :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-2b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-2b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =2\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-2b) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 30\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186532648 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=(2Ax+b)^{T}(2Ax+b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [17592186532648 :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=2Ax+b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2Ax+b)=2A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & =(2A)^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & =(2A)^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =4\\cdot {A}^{T}(2Ax+b) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}(2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}](2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-24 \\\\ -68\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186532650 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186532650 :question/worked-solution "Writing $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}Ax.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2Ax.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186532651 :question/problem "What is the sum of the series $$\n\\displaystyle 1-\\frac{e}{4}+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{16}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}{e}^{n}}{{4}^{n}}+⋯\\,?\n$$"] [17592186532652 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $$\n\\displaystyle x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186532652 :question/worked-solution "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2+1+1 \\\\ 1+2-2 \\\\ 1-2+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186532659 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}{-(n+1)}^{2}.\n$$"] [17592186532660 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $$\n\\displaystyle x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186532660 :question/worked-solution "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4-3 \\\\ 3+4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186532661 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186532661 :question/worked-solution "Writing $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}Ax.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2Ax.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186532664 :question/problem "Using the standard form of the Maclaurin series of ${e}^{x},$ find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion of the definite integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1-{e}^{-x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186532665 :question/problem "Find the sum $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2}}{2!}+⋯+\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{n}}{n!}+⋯.\n$$"] [17592186532668 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{4}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186532668 :question/worked-solution "Writing $$\n\\displaystyle b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={b}^{T}x.\n$$\n\nThe gradient of a function $f(x)={b}^{T}x$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=b.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186532671 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186532671 :question/worked-solution "Writing $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}Ax.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2Ax.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186532674 :question/problem "Find the sum of the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 1-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{162}+⋯+\\frac{(-\\pi )^{n}}{{3}^{n}n!}+⋯=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186532679 :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $2\\pm 3\\text{i}.$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${e}^{At}={e}^{2t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"140px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"140px\">$2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>"] [17592186532683 :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}(\\frac{n}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})\n$$ as a sum of four terms."] [17592186532685 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{50}+\\frac{1}{375}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n{5}^{n}}+⋯=\n$$"] [17592186532686 :question/problem "Using the standard form of the Maclaurin series of $\\frac{1}{1-x},$ find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion of the definite integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{4}{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186532687 :question/problem "Find the sum of the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 1+\\frac{5}{9}+\\frac{25}{81}+⋯+\\frac{{5}^{n}}{{9}^{n}}+⋯=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186532689 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $$\n\\displaystyle x={[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186532689 :question/worked-solution "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0+0 \\\\ 0+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186532691 :question/problem "Starting with the standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion for $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186532692 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $$\n\\displaystyle x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186532692 :question/worked-solution "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6-8 \\\\ -4+4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186532693 :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(2,0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4x$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4x \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =4\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/df/dfc7359ad44caa959459676a1e54bf1575e3c7e0f771e92a74817d9d1f9cb581.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\n$$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$"] [17592186532694 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /4}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{4}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy.\n$$"] [17592186532695 :question/problem "Find the sum of the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 1-\\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{4}{49}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{2}^{n}}{{7}^{n}}+⋯=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186532697 :question/problem "Find the sum $$\n\\displaystyle 1-\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{50}-\\frac{1}{750}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{5}^{n}n!}+⋯.\n$$"] [17592186532702 :question/problem "Find the sum of the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 1+\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{{4}^{2}2!}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{{4}^{3}3!}+⋯+\\frac{{\\pi }^{n}}{{4}^{n}n!}+⋯=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186532704 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=2(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [17592186532704 :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)=2{y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{T}y)=4{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 4y \\\\  & =4\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-b) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 16 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-68 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186532708 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=(5Ax-b)^{T}(5Ax-b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [17592186532708 :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=5Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5Ax-b)=5A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & =(5A)^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & =(5A)^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =10\\cdot {A}^{T}(5Ax-b) \\\\  & =10\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}(5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =10\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}](5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =10\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =10\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =10\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 120 \\\\ 180\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186532715 :question/problem "![](images/c2/c27ab9b0062fabfe1fdbfd43631bf1f143c0fa4ec80587519e9ce6c4e84e7b2b.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\n$$ shown above."] [17592186532719 :question/problem "Find the sum $$\n\\displaystyle 1-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{18}-\\frac{1}{162}+⋯+\\frac{(-1/3)^{n}}{n!}+⋯\\,.\n$$"] [17592186532721 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle 1+\\frac{1}{7}+\\frac{1}{49}+⋯+\\frac{1}{{7}^{n}}+⋯=\n$$"] [17592186532723 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}x{(1+x)}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186532724 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186532724 :question/worked-solution "Writing $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}Ax.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2Ax.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186532725 :question/problem "Starting with the standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion for $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186532783 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-6\\sin\\left(-\\frac{7}{8}\\left(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)\\right).\n$$"] [17592186532783 :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nThe coefficient of $x$ in the argument is $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\left(-\\frac{7}{8}\\right)(4)=-\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{7}{2}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{7}{2}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{7}{2}}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7}.\n$$"] [17592186532784 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-6\\cos\\left(-\\frac{7}{5}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).\n$$"] [17592186532784 :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{7}{5}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{7}{5}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{7}{5}}=\\frac{10\\pi }{7}.\n$$"] [17592186532792 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-4\\cos\\left(-\\frac{2}{5}x+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right).\n$$"] [17592186532792 :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{2}{5}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{2}{5}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{2}{5}}=5\\pi .\n$$"] [17592186532798 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=6\\sin\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right).\n$$"] [17592186532798 :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{1}{4}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{1}{4}}=8\\pi .\n$$"] [17592186532803 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=6\\sin\\left(-\\frac{1}{6}\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\right).\n$$"] [17592186532803 :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nThe coefficient of $x$ in the argument is $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\left(-\\frac{1}{6}\\right)(2)=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{1}{3}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{1}{3}}=6\\pi .\n$$"] [17592186532817 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=2\\cos\\left(-\\frac{11}{10}\\left(4x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\right).\n$$"] [17592186532817 :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nThe coefficient of $x$ in the argument is $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\left(-\\frac{11}{10}\\right)(4)=-\\frac{22}{5}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{22}{5}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{22}{5}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{22}{5}}=\\frac{5\\pi }{11}.\n$$"] [17592186532819 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-6\\sin\\left(-\\frac{9}{5}x+\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right).\n$$"] [17592186532819 :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{9}{5}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{9}{5}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{9}{5}}=\\frac{10\\pi }{9}.\n$$"] [17592186532825 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-6\\sin\\left(-\\frac{11}{10}\\left(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\right).\n$$"] [17592186532825 :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nThe coefficient of $x$ in the argument is $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\left(-\\frac{11}{10}\\right)(5)=-\\frac{11}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{11}{2}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{11}{2}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{11}{2}}=\\frac{4\\pi }{11}.\n$$"] [17592186532829 :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|\\frac{1}{4}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{1}{4}}=8\\pi .\n$$"] [17592186532831 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-4\\cos\\left(-\\frac{1}{10}\\left(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\right).\n$$"] [17592186532831 :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nThe coefficient of $x$ in the argument is $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\left(-\\frac{1}{10}\\right)(4)=-\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{2}{5}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{2}{5}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{2}{5}}=5\\pi .\n$$"] [17592186532843 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-3\\cos\\left(-\\frac{1}{6}x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right).\n$$"] [17592186532843 :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{1}{6}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{1}{6}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{1}{6}}=12\\pi .\n$$"] [17592186532876 :question/problem "Find the value of $(3h(t) - 2g(t))^{′}$ at $t = 2$, if\n$$\n\\displaystyle h(t)= ⟨6 t^{2},\\,3 - t,\\,3 t^{3} + 24⟩; g(t)= ⟨3 t \\left(t + 2\\right),\\,-8,\\,6 t^{3}⟩\n$$"] [17592186532877 :question/problem "Find the value of $(3h(t) - 2g(t))^{′}$ at $t = 2$, if\n$$\n\\displaystyle h(t)= ⟨2 t^{2},\\,9 - 3 t,\\,- 2 t^{3} - 16⟩; g(t)= ⟨t \\left(t + 2\\right),\\,-24,\\,- 4 t^{3}⟩\n$$"] [17592186533973 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-S\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$ \n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0.6+1.8+0.7+2.9+1.5 \\\\  & =7.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{5}\\,{e}^{-7.5\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186533974 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=5.2,\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}=22.6,\n$$ and $n=6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-6/2}\\,{e}^{-(22.6-2(5.2)\\theta +6{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8{\\pi }^{3}}\\,{e}^{\\,-11.3\\,+\\,5.2\\theta \\,-\\,3{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-11.3+5.2\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$"] [17592186533976 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4n(n+1)}=\n$$"] [17592186533979 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n(n+1)}=\n$$"] [17592186533981 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{3n(n+1)}=\n$$"] [17592186533986 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=3({y}^{T}Ax)$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [17592186533986 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}3{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}=3{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=3(y\\cdot {x}^{T}).$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=3([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5\\end{aligned}])=3([\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 15\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 30 \\\\ -3 & -15 \\\\ 9 & 45\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186533992 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X)=(det(X))^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $$\n\\displaystyle X=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [17592186533992 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of a function $f:{R}^{m\\times n}\\to R$ with respect to a matrix $X\\in {R}^{m\\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{mn}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $$\n\\displaystyle X=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{2\\times 2},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\nf(X)=(det(X))^{2}=({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot {x}_{22} & 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot (-{x}_{21}) \\\\ 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot (-{x}_{12}) & 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot {x}_{11}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2det(X)[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle X=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=2(4\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}14 & -42 \\\\ 14 & 56\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186533993 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(det(X)).\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $$\n\\displaystyle X=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [17592186533993 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of a function $f:{R}^{m\\times n}\\to R$ with respect to a matrix $X\\in {R}^{m\\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{mn}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $$\n\\displaystyle X=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{2\\times 2},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\nf(X)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(det(X))=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}).\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}))\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}_{22}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{{x}_{21}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} \\\\ \\frac{{x}_{12}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{-{x}_{11}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle X=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{-4-(-3)} & \\frac{1}{-4-(-3)} \\\\ \\frac{-3}{-4-(-3)} & -\\frac{(-1)}{-4-(-3)}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{-1} & \\frac{1}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{-3}{-1} & -\\frac{(-1)}{-1}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186533994 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=13$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 12\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186533995 :question/problem "Evaluate the series\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(n+3)(n+4)}.\n$$\nDo not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{(n+3)(n+4)}=\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+4}.\n$$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(n+3)(n+4)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186533998 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{9-2x}{x(x-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186533999 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186534000 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)={y}^{T}Ax$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [17592186534000 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle y=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186534003 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{x}_{ij}^{2}\n$$\n\n \nwhere ${x}_{ij}$ are the corresponding matrix's entries. What is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $$\n\\displaystyle X=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [17592186534003 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of a function $f:{R}^{m\\times n}\\to R$ with respect to a matrix $X\\in {R}^{m\\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{mn}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $$\n\\displaystyle X=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{2\\times 2},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\nf(X)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{x}_{ij}^{2}=({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{11} & 2{x}_{12} \\\\ 2{x}_{21} & 2{x}_{22}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2X.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle X=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (1) & 2\\cdot (5) \\\\ 2\\cdot (-3) & 2\\cdot (4)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186534008 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 3}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=3({y}^{T}Ax)$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [17592186534008 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}3{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}=3{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=3(y\\cdot {x}^{T}).$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=3([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 7\\end{aligned}])=3([\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 & 14 \\\\ 10 & -15 & -35\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 18 & 42 \\\\ 30 & -45 & -105\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186534009 :question/problem "Evaluate the series\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}-6n+8}.\n$$\nDo not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Try to factor the denominator first.*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}-6n+8}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186534013 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-6$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $$\n\\displaystyle [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-6t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-6t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={e}^{-6t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $$\n\\displaystyle [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{-6t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $$\n\\displaystyle [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-6t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/ff/ff337e0120343c7c956afa5e49ea3b482fbd79fe363783a018842d1bfeb340d6.png)"] [17592186534016 :question/problem "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{(n+3)(n+5)}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{(n+3)(n+5)}=\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+5}).\n$$*"] [17592186534018 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+8x+11}{(x+6)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186534022 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=\\frac{1}{2}(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"8px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"45px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"45px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"8px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-35$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186534022 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b\\in {R}^{3}.$ Then, $L(A)=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{3}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y)=y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}={y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}={x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{3}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =(Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =([\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -70 \\\\ 0 & -35 \\\\ 0 & 63\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186534024 :question/problem "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{T-3}+\\frac{5}{T-4}-\\frac{5}{{T}^{2}-7T+12}=0.\n$$"] [17592186534027 :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=7}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n-3)(n-2)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186534028 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=8}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{n(n+1)}=\n$$"] [17592186534029 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)={y}^{T}Ax$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [17592186534029 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle y=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-40 & 10 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186534030 :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+2)(n+1)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186534033 :question/problem "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{(n+2)(n+4)}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{(n+2)(n+4)}=\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n+2}-\\frac{1}{n+4}).\n$$*"] [17592186534034 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=2({y}^{T}Ax)$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [17592186534034 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}2{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}=2{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=2(y{x}^{T}).$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle y=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=2([\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 7\\end{aligned}])=2([\\begin{aligned}-18 & 42 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-36 & 84 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 14\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186534039 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{8x-3}{5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}} $-\\,\\frac{23}{25}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $)$"] [17592186534045 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=9,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=9:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-9I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186534046 :question/problem "Evaluate the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=8}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}-7n+12}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Try to factor the denominator first.*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=8}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}-7n+12}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186534047 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=8,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=5,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=3,$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186534054 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=16$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=-13.2,\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}=33.2,\n$$ and $n=16.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-16/2}\\,{e}^{-(33.2-2(-13.2)\\theta +16{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{256{\\pi }^{8}}\\,{e}^{\\,-16.6\\,-\\,13.2\\theta \\,-\\,8{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-16.6-13.2\\theta -8{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$"] [17592186534057 :question/problem "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+4n+3}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Try to factor the denominator first.*"] [17592186534065 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{3}+x-1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186534070 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{x-\\theta }{\\sigma }\\right)}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =1.7+2.3+2.2+1.1+1.9+1.8 \\\\  & =11 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(1.7)^{2}+(2.3)^{2}+(2.2)^{2}+(1.1)^{2}+(1.9)^{2}+(1.8)^{2} \\\\  & =21.08\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-6/2}\\,{e}^{-(21.08-2(11)\\theta +6{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8{\\pi }^{3}}\\,{e}^{\\,-10.54\\,+\\,11\\theta \\,-\\,3{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-10.54+11\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$"] [17592186534071 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $v=[1,\\text{ }-1]^{T}$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{{field-1}}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}{{field-2}}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-2t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/45/4522e4e6ed382e821aaa47d40a191135161f95eef276b6e8ba3a8514e6bfdfd8.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y=(-t+1){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | {{field-7}} | $0$ | $0.18$ |\n| $y$ | {{field-8}} | $1$ | $0.18$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/db/dbf5a13ecd5e81c399204fd29d235e410fcb62274555cf3e5b32a383c1078d24.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/a6/a680b8c9319290df3beb20d7db964f385b368b01ead33961e2c10d22b19db4eb.png)"] [17592186534071 :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/45/4522e4e6ed382e821aaa47d40a191135161f95eef276b6e8ba3a8514e6bfdfd8.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{-2t} \\\\ y=(-t+1){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-1.36$ | $0$ | $0.18$ |\n| $y$ | $4.08$ | $1$ | $0.18$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/db/dbf5a13ecd5e81c399204fd29d235e410fcb62274555cf3e5b32a383c1078d24.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/13/13a57f14ea65118473c24c6eb1dbf50a0461060201ced051bb2eb6776f5daf64.png)"] [17592186534073 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$20$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$36$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186534073 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b\\in {R}^{2}.$ Then, $L(A)={y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y)=2y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}=2{y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}={x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}{x}_{j}..\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is $2$ times the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=2y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =2y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =2(Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =2([\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 20 \\\\ -18 & 36\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186534074 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-3}^{-2}\\frac{5-3x}{{x}^{2}+2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186534080 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"8px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"45px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"45px\">$0$</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"8px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\">$-32$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186534080 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b\\in {R}^{3}.$ Then, $L(A)={y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{3}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y)=2y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}=2{y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}={x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{3}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is $2$ times the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=2y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =2y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =2(Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =2([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2([\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -32 & -32 \\\\ 16 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186534082 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 3}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=(2Ax-b)^{T}(2Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$-240$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2140$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186534082 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=2Ax-b\\in {R}^{2}.$ Then, $L(A)={y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y)=2y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}=2{y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=2Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}=2{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=2{x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot 2{x}_{j} \\\\  & =4{y}_{i}{x}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n The $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is $4$ times the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=4y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\n Finally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =4y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =4(2Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =4(2[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 10 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4([\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 110\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 10 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}48 \\\\ 428\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 10 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}480 & 480 & -240 \\\\ 4280 & 4280 & -2140\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186534094 :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{n(n+1)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186534095 :question/problem "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}-3n+2}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Try to factor the denominator first.*"] [17592186534098 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}-4x+4}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186534178 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $$\n\\displaystyle r(t)=\\langle 6 t + 4,- \\frac{\\left(3 t + 2\\right)^{3}}{3} - \\frac{1}{3 t + 2}\\rangle\n$$ from $t=0$ to $t=1$.\n\n![](images/db/db3e582e3cbf491015aa1f08ac77ddb6b02b2a8143bebc2d76d171ebd64f9570.png)"] [17592186534188 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $$\n\\displaystyle r(t)=\\langle 4 t + 2,- \\frac{\\left(2 t + 1\\right)^{3}}{3} - \\frac{1}{2 t + 1}\\rangle\n$$ from $t=0$ to $t=1$.\n\n![](images/8d/8d83a591169c9ac41f6637a985554b0b907fa017349bf92c9197618a3f61409b.png)"] [17592186534217 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle R=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 7 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 7\\end{aligned}],\n$$ then $det(R)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186534218 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle Q=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -7 & 8 \\\\ 7 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 7 & -5 & 6\\end{aligned}],\n$$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186534226 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle H=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}],\n$$ then $det(H)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186534228 :question/problem "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}10 & -8 & -9 \\\\ -11 & 12 & -7 \\\\ 12 & 7 & 12\\end{aligned}|.\n$$"] [17592186534229 :question/problem "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 & -6 \\\\ -7 & 5 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\n$$"] [17592186534234 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}],\n$$ then $det(A)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186534237 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle Q=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & 8 & -7 \\\\ -10 & -5 & -12\\end{aligned}],\n$$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186534244 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle Q=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 9 & 8 & -4 \\\\ -12 & -9 & -10\\end{aligned}],\n$$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186534246 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle Q=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -7 & 7 \\\\ 12 & -12 & 5\\end{aligned}],\n$$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186534251 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle W=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 9 & 8 \\\\ 12 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 8\\end{aligned}],\n$$ then $det(W)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186534974 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}-\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{y}{x}\\,dy+dz,\n$$ where $C$ is a portion of the helix parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$"] [17592186534979 :question/problem "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n+1}}{(9+5n)^{n}}.$ According to the root test, does $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ converge or diverge?"] [17592186534979 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n+1}}{(9+5n)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n+1}}{(9+5n)^{n}}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n}\\cdot n}{(9+5n)^{n}}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5n\\sqrt[n]{n}}{9+5n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\sqrt[n]{n}\\cdot \\frac{5n}{n})}{(\\frac{9}{n}+\\frac{5n}{n})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\sqrt[n]{n}}{(\\frac{9}{n}+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 1}{0+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{n}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=1,$ the root test gives no conclusion."] [17592186534981 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy+z\\,dz,\n$$ where $C$ is a portion of the curve $r(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]?$"] [17592186534990 :question/worked-solution "![](images/1f/1ffb0a83abf7e736c47035f4f337c36a6bb129fd0d64fe0ac3bfe8e46c194a11.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },\n$$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"] [17592186534994 :question/problem "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{11}^{n}}{{7}^{n}\\cdot n!}.$ Does $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ converge or diverge?"] [17592186534994 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{11}^{n}}{{7}^{n}\\cdot n!}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{11}^{n}}{{7}^{n}\\cdot n!}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{11}{7\\cdot \\sqrt[n]{n!}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{7}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{\\frac{1}{n!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{7}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{\\frac{1}{n!}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince $L=0<1,$ the series converges by the root test."] [17592186534996 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ]?$"] [17592186534998 :question/problem "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{n/2}}{3{n}^{{n}^{2}}}.$ Does $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ converge or diverge?"] [17592186534998 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{n/2}}{3{n}^{{n}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{n}^{n/2}}{3{n}^{{n}^{2}}}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt[n]{3}\\cdot {n}^{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{\\frac{1}{3}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{n}^{n-1/2}} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{\\frac{1}{3}}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=0<1,$ the series converges by the root test."] [17592186535000 :question/problem "![](images/a5/a5f23af5dd514a65937eee29bb0992ff94d68897d8c6422fcf215aad45379c4a.png)\n\nConsider the part of parabola and constant vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the part of parabola is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$"] [17592186535000 :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I is false, while statement II is true. As the particle moves from $A$ to $B,$ the force $F$ works with the particle for $y<0,$ while $F$  works against the particle for $y>0.$ \n\n![](images/53/537aca6190fc2f3e06e85c0781a8bb461559ef0a7515ee29aca505f9d615f820.png)\n\n \n\nBy symmetry, the contribution to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for $y<0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $y>0.$ So,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/49/4939f9a22400a7a56eaeacb77d520e522f50aa8378ca1014a19810122e12da51.png)\n\n \n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186535011 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(-2) \\\\ y(-2)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"130px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186535011 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$ So, the matrix of the system is $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t+2)}\\,x(-2) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(t+2)) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5(t+2)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5(t+2)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(t+2))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(t+2))\\, \\\\ \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5(t+2))\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186535015 :question/problem "![](images/dc/dcf7a2ea5b44f3f5ba7b1663f7979cdc3fc8c1036b6cc797904ffda77f1003bd.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the сurve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$"] [17592186535015 :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$  assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/81/818ad935fd7aa592f00c23c9c77799d127449b4bb6ffacee4354e7acf39887d8.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/70/70e268a08195a92573f41927ae7de653837fca273f0c75cb951243a31ec660a2.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186535021 :question/worked-solution "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and collect the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100{x}^{2} & =7 \\\\ 100{x}^{2}-7 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the difference of squares formula, we factor the left-hand side of our quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100{x}^{2}-7 & =0 \\\\ (10x)^{2}-{(\\sqrt{7})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (10x-\\sqrt{7})(10x+\\sqrt{7}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\n10x-\\sqrt{7}=0\\,\\text{or}\\,10x+\\sqrt{7}=0.\n$$\n\nThen, we solve each equation separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x-\\sqrt{7} & =0 & \\, & \\, & 10x+\\sqrt{7} & =0 \\\\ 10x & =\\sqrt{7} & \\, & \\, & 10x & =-\\sqrt{7} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10} & \\, & \\, & x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $$\n\\displaystyle x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10},\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10}.\n$$ We can write this even more concisely as $x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10}.$"] [17592186535023 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,i+j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ]?$"] [17592186535039 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{4}{{z}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$"] [17592186535053 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer. We discard all solutions except $x=\\pi ,$ since it is the only value that belongs to the given interval.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\pi $ and the endpoints $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0 \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=1 \\\\ f\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(\\pi )=1,$ so the global maximum is $1.$\n\n- The least value is $$\n\\displaystyle f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=f\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,\n$$ so the global minimum is $0.$\n\n![](images/7e/7e0008d35355afebc392a4bc508bd2cf2de5dc32a108f9f9c030d75a24ece076.png)"] [17592186535055 :question/problem "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(10n)^{n}n}{(8n+1)^{n}}.$ According to the root test, does $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ converge or diverge?"] [17592186535055 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(10n)^{n}n}{(8n+1)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{(10n)^{n}n}{(8n+1)^{n}}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{10n\\sqrt[n]{n}}{8n+1}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{10n\\sqrt[n]{n}}{n(8+\\frac{1}{n})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{10\\sqrt[n]{n}}{(8+\\frac{1}{n})} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 1}{8+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{n}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=\\frac{5}{4}>1,$ the series diverges by the root test."] [17592186535057 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}-y\\,dx+x\\,dy-z\\,dz,\n$$ where $C$ is a portion of the helix parametrized by $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$"] [17592186535064 :question/worked-solution "![](images/df/df5d54940a136a53c1e1c00f8cc9d71d7b064f6e0895a029da2738cf21998a56.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ and $y=\\frac{1}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },\n$$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}=2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$"] [17592186535069 :question/problem "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{6}^{n}}{{5}^{n}{n}^{6}}.$ According to the root test, does $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ converge or diverge?"] [17592186535069 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{6}^{n}}{{5}^{n}{n}^{6}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{6}^{n}}{{5}^{n}{n}^{6}}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{6}{5\\cdot \\sqrt[n]{{n}^{6}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[n]{n}}\\right)}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot {1}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{n}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=\\frac{6}{5}>1,$ the series diverges by the root test."] [17592186535072 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}xy\\,dx+yz\\,dy+z\\,dz,\n$$ where $C$ is a portion of the line $r(t)=t\\,i+5t\\,j+\\,k$ for $t\\in [2,3]?$"] [17592186535078 :question/problem "![](images/8a/8aed400e7768b70f4532642418d83b36d9e32aa84db87c71ea6d902504930bc4.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the semiellipse is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$"] [17592186535078 :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/d8/d8e05de34bf9ddfdbf9580b1df32eb9e091f56f76ac3616cbb1cd81d5d212a86.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/17/17423916013677c1476df5a4368ab9eec81671b04b4ca29a0e702b20ddc818f2.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186535079 :question/problem "![](images/3c/3c2035d50b823b9bcbeb3ae8cf698df9a25c95a289c5a6f35054269dc920088d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$"] [17592186535079 :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/df/dfec76b47e8508a57e18440a72986bcae8e2bd19559bd0915ea4530e0b08b4f6.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/58/58e3efc39d0aee02d14e7c80c7355c37a1f28b6f0f54ff7102553e3a6ffc17cf.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186535087 :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{6t}z(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{6t}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-6)t}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $$\n\\displaystyle Z(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-6)t}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t=Z(s-6),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-6>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+6.\n$$"] [17592186535088 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{4}{3}{y}^{7}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$"] [17592186535305 :question/problem "![](images/38/38947ad6e5f10e1e25d3d10777ad28c47aac1f0c0f311621c0194bef815331ab.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D 2 x y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?\n\nChoose a change-of-variables setup with $r\\geq0$ that describes $D$ and the transformed integrand, including the Jacobian."] [17592186535318 :question/problem "![](images/b4/b4a332a1bea58e47ab58fa47c6944213ce4caa5d26e6bfb129fea613adcd524b.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D 2 x y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?\n\nChoose a change-of-variables setup with $r\\geq0$ that describes $D$ and the transformed integrand, including the Jacobian."] [17592186535320 :question/problem "![](images/66/66c8e80f89c7964cae5443f9e3cb04916ec6aee3e16f27480d8d85d3a344187e.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D \\left(x^{2} - y^{2}\\right) \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?\n\nChoose a change-of-variables setup with $r\\geq0$ that describes $D$ and the transformed integrand, including the Jacobian."] [17592186535330 :question/problem "![](images/7b/7bd61d778e3ea17a10adad21dd63cfae068af48700e6217194bb48e995c9f6e7.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?\n\nChoose a change-of-variables setup with $r\\geq0$ that describes $D$ and the transformed integrand, including the Jacobian."] [17592186535332 :question/problem "![](images/66/66c8e80f89c7964cae5443f9e3cb04916ec6aee3e16f27480d8d85d3a344187e.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D 2 x y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?\n\nChoose a change-of-variables setup with $r\\geq0$ that describes $D$ and the transformed integrand, including the Jacobian."] [17592186535337 :question/problem "![](images/b4/b4a332a1bea58e47ab58fa47c6944213ce4caa5d26e6bfb129fea613adcd524b.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D \\left(x^{2} - y^{2}\\right) \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?\n\nChoose a change-of-variables setup with $r\\geq0$ that describes $D$ and the transformed integrand, including the Jacobian."] [17592186535342 :question/problem "![](images/7b/7bd61d778e3ea17a10adad21dd63cfae068af48700e6217194bb48e995c9f6e7.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?\n\nChoose a change-of-variables setup with $r\\geq0$ that describes $D$ and the transformed integrand, including the Jacobian."] [17592186535386 :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\cdot -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$"] [17592186535927 :question/worked-solution "A dilation with center at the origin and a factor of $k$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(kx,ky)\\,.\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the point $P$ and equating the result to ${P}^{′},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k\\cdot (-4),k\\cdot (-2)=(3,1.5)\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-4k=3 \\\\ -2k=1.5.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle k=\\frac{3}{-4}=\\frac{1.5}{-2}=-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$   \n\n![](images/d3/d3b818151bcfddd3fd4f642463ba4e06ba1844d4b772652dadde0d3049e441ee.png)"] [17592186535937 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{MN}$ traversed from the point $M(1,3)$ to the point $N(3,-1),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{e}^{x-y}\\,dx+{e}^{x-y}\\,dy=\n$$"] [17592186535938 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186535950 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"120px\">$2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186535950 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$ So, the matrix of the system is $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})} & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}]}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186535981 :question/problem "Consider the inhomogeneous system  ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. A derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x(0)$ is presented below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let $y(t)={e}^{-At}x(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${y}^{′}(t)=-A{e}^{-At}x(t)+{e}^{-At}{x}^{′}(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${y}^{′}(t)={e}^{-At}g(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle y(t)-y(0)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-As}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s\n$$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $y(0)=x(0)$ \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle y(t)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-As}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s\n$$  \n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}y(t)={e}^{At}x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s\n$$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${e}^{At}y(t)=x(t)$\n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)={e}^{At}x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by  \n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by  \n{{field-3}}."] [17592186535993 :question/worked-solution "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle \\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}\n$$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (2+3+3+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ \\overset{―}{y} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (28+20+24+16) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 88 \\\\  & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(2\\,-\\,3)(28\\,-\\,22)+(3\\,-\\,3)(20\\,-\\,22)+(3\\,-\\,3)(24\\,-\\,22)+(4\\,-\\,3)(16\\,-\\,22)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-1)(6)+(0)(-2)+(0)(2)+(1)(-6)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[-6+0+0+(-6)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186535999 :question/worked-solution "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle \\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}\n$$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(2-10)(-2-(-1))+(14-10)(-1-(-1)) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,+(18-10)(-3-(-1))+(6-10)(2-(-1))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-8)(-1)+(4)(0)+(8)(-2)+(-4)(3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[8+0-16-12] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-20) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186536002 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{MN}$ traversed from the point $M(1,1)$ to the point $N(3,3),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy=\n$$"] [17592186536251 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,y=4 t + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\n$$\nfrom $t=-1$ to $t=1$?"] [17592186536259 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,y=6 t + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\n$$\nfrom $t=-1$ to $t=1$?"] [17592186536262 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,y=5 t + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\n$$\nfrom $t=-1$ to $t=1$?"] [17592186536806 :multistep/context "![](images/2b/2bbc3e9ce241640af48af50c7d0e0c2c4120b0fc16a00fbc7837ecaeab6bbd9a.png)\n\n \n\nA function $f(x)$ is infinitely differentiable for all real values of its argument $x.$ The graph of $y=f(x)$ and the tangent line to it at the point where $x=0$ are shown above. A few selected derivatives of $f(x)$ at $x=0$ are also given in the table below.\n\n| $n$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{(n)}(0)$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ | $3$ |\n\nIn addition, the function $g$ is defined as \n\n$$\ng(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFurthermore, it is known that the coefficients of the terms of the Maclaurin series for $g(x)$ form a convergent alternating series $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.\n$$"] [17592186536814 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{5\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186536820 :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=(x+1)^{2}$ between $x=-1$ and $x=3$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a right Riemann sum using $4$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the right Riemann sum ${S}_{R}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $i.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {S}_{R}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186536821 :question/problem "Find the value of ${a}_{5},$ the fifth nonzero term of the alternating series $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.\n$$\n\n${a}_{5}\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186536821 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the terms of our series $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ are the coefficients of the Maclaurin series for $g(x).$\n\nPreviously, we found the fourth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x):$\n\n$$\nf(x)≈{P}_{4}(x)=2-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{4}\n$$\n\nTo find the ${x}^{5}$ term of the Maclaurin series of $g(x),$ we simply need to integrate the ${x}^{4}$ term of ${P}_{4}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}{t}^{5}\\,{|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}{x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${a}_{5}$ is the coefficient of $\\frac{1}{40}{x}^{5},$ that is, ${a}_{5}=\\frac{1}{40}.$"] [17592186536823 :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{7}\\frac{2}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{7}\\frac{2}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}]$"] [17592186536829 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{5}8{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{5}8{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[8(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]$"] [17592186536831 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-4}^{-1}\\sqrt{5}{x}^{-4}\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?"] [17592186536832 :question/problem "![](images/50/5034bb902aadd151cc1a28764e054dbdd6a694ad653f50557e5fc208c8ec2342.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi $ (shown in blue in the diagram above).\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the following identity: $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}.\n$$*"] [17592186536837 :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[3(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]$"] [17592186536844 :question/worked-solution "We have that\n\n$$\nf(x)≈{P}_{3}(x)=2-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate $$\n\\displaystyle g(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ by integrating the above polynomial term-by-term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ≈{\\int }_{0}^{x}(2-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[2t-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{24}{t}^{4}]}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =[2x-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4}]-0 \\\\  & =2x-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $g(1)$ using the approximation above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1) & ≈2(1)-\\frac{1}{4}(1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{12}(1)^{3}-\\frac{1}{24}(1)^{4} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{12}-\\frac{1}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186536849 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {sech}^{4}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186536852 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{5}2{x}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?"] [17592186536861 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}(x+8)^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}(x+8)^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]$"] [17592186536874 :question/problem "Let $F(x,y,z)=yz\\,i+xz\\,j+xy\\,k$ and let $C$ be a path in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?\n$$"] [17592186536887 :question/problem "![](images/7d/7d7a51344b945f502ace0aa7b9d5dee46514a465adda7666d83fe91bd2ffb3a2.png)\n\nThe area under the curve $y=\\sqrt{x+1}$ between $x=0$, $x=4$ and the $x$-axis is approximated using a left Riemann sum, as shown above. What is the expression for the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $k.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {S}_{L}={\\sum }_{k=0}^{3}\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186536900 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186536901 :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+1)$ between $x=-1$ and $x=2$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a right Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the right Riemann sum ${S}_{R}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $j$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {S}_{R}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\frac{3}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\n$$\n{{field-1}} $)$"] [17592186537879 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{1-{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186537880 :question/problem "The function $f(x)={log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{5x-1}{k}\\right)$ has an inverse function given by $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{3}^{x+2}}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$ What is the value of $k?$"] [17592186537880 :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{5x-1}{k}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{5y-1}{k}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{5y-1}{k}\\right) \\\\ {3}^{{log}_{3}⁡((5y-1)/k)} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{5y-1}{k} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ 5y-1 & ={3}^{x}\\cdot k \\\\ 5y & ={3}^{x}\\cdot k+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{{3}^{x}\\cdot k}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{3}^{x}\\cdot k}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k={3}^{2}=9.$"] [17592186537881 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({cosh}^{3}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186537882 :question/problem "The inverse of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1\n$$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186537885 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2{cosh}^{2}⁡2x+2{sinh}^{2}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186537886 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-16{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186537887 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}({cosh}^{2}⁡x+3)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n {{field-2}}\n$)\\,+\\,$\n {{field-3}}"] [17592186537888 :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}$ by *adding* integer multiples of $2\\pi $ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n- $-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}+2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{5}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{11\\pi }{5}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{9\\pi }{5}\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo the angle $-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}$ is coterminal with $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/4a/4a6bb0dd4991fe1a00bfedc7814f27c4a36b4114fa027673ed9cd03856fcc0d5.png)\n\nWe see that $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, $-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}$ also lies in this quadrant."] [17592186537890 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (2{cosh}^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4{x}^{3}-2{sinh}^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186537891 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (4{sinh}^{2}⁡2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186537892 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}({cosh}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{2}\\right)+1)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n{{field-2}}\n$)+\\,$\n{{field-3}}"] [17592186537895 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+1)+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-1))\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186537896 :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2x-k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2y-k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2y-k} \\\\ {5}^{2y-k} & =\\frac{2}{3}-x \\\\ {5}^{2y-k} & =\\frac{2-3x}{3} \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡({5}^{2y-k}) & ={log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{2-3x}{3}\\right) \\\\ 2y-k & ={log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{2-3x}{3}\\right) \\\\ 2y & ={log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{2-3x}{3}\\right)+k \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{2-3x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{k}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{2-3x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{k}{2}\n$$  \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=0.$"] [17592186537898 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186537900 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186537902 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{{coth}^{4}⁡x-1}{1+{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186537909 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{sinh}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-4)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n{{field-2}}\n$)-\\,$\n{{field-3}}"] [17592186537912 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{sech}^{2}⁡x}{1-{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186537915 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{2+2{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186537923 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(4-3{cosh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,-\\,\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n{{field-2}}\n$)$"] [17592186537925 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (1-{sinh}^{2}⁡x{sech}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186537926 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186537927 :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{19\\pi }{4}$ by *subtracting* integer multiples of $2\\pi $ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n- $\\frac{19\\pi }{4}-2\\pi =\\frac{11\\pi }{4}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{19\\pi }{4}-2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo the angle $\\frac{19\\pi }{4}$ is coterminal with $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/d0/d00b1363ef23bfd1e84ae968e1dc6ec4e36aa7f45d469c653b9f930cb0705cdf.png)\n\nWe see that $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ lies in quadrant II. Therefore, $\\frac{19\\pi }{4}$ also lies in this quadrant."] [17592186537928 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{7}\\frac{{coth}^{4}⁡x-{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,{coth}^{2}⁡x}{{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186537929 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x{tanh}^{2}⁡x}{{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186537930 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{cosh}^{3}⁡(2x-1)}{1+{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186537934 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/3}(2{sinh}^{2}⁡3x+2{cosh}^{2}⁡3x-1)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186537938 :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{x-2}{2x}+\\frac{x-4}{2x}=\\frac{5x-6}{2x}.\n$$"] [17592186537943 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{{sech}^{2}⁡x}{1-{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186537944 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+{csch}^{3}⁡x}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186537949 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{2-2{tanh}^{4}⁡x}{1+{tanh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186537952 :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $, we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi :$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi $ to $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\pi =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{14\\pi }{3}\\,✓\n$$\n\nOf the given values, there is no angle greater than $\\frac{14\\pi }{3}.$ So, we can stop there.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi $ from $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$:\n\n- $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\pi =-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\cdot (2\\pi )=-\\frac{10\\pi }{3}\\,✓\n$$\n\nOf the given angles, all three are coterminal with $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nSo the correct answer is I, II, and III."] [17592186537956 :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $ (in radians), we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi :$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi $ to $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$:\n\n- $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}+2\\pi =\\frac{19\\pi }{5}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{9\\pi }{5}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{29\\pi }{5}\\,✓\n$$\n\nThere are no answer choices larger than $\\frac{29\\pi }{5},$ so we can stop here.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi $ from $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$:\n\n- $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{5}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{9\\pi }{5}-2\\cdot (2\\pi )=-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{9\\pi }{5}-3\\cdot (2\\pi )=-\\frac{21\\pi }{5}\n$$\n\nThere are no answer choices smaller than $-\\frac{21\\pi }{5},$ so we can stop here.\n\nSo the correct answer is $\\frac{29\\pi }{5}.$"] [17592186537957 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{2}{5+20{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186537963 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round your answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(6+6{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186537967 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{6}{1+{sinh}^{2}⁡(3x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186537970 :question/problem "Evaluate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(3-2{cosh}^{2}⁡3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186537971 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round your answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(2-12{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186537973 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡7x(2-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186537975 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\frac{1+{csch}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186537979 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186537982 :question/problem "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x(1-4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186537984 :question/problem "Which value of $m$ satisfies the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{4m+1}{3m}-\\frac{m-1}{6m}=\\frac{1}{9m}?\n$$"] [17592186537985 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{csch}^{3}⁡(7x-2)+\\operatorname{csch}⁡(7x-2)}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡(7x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186537986 :question/problem "The inverse of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186537987 :question/problem "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(2+3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186537988 :question/problem "Which value of $n$ satisfies the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2n}-\\frac{1-n}{{n}^{2}}=\\frac{7}{3n}?\n$$"] [17592186537990 :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{kx}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{ky}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{ky}+2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{ky} & =x-2 \\\\ {3}^{ky} & =\\frac{5x-10}{2} \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{ky}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5) \\\\ ky & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{k}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{k}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5).\n$$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=1.$"] [17592186537991 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{x{coth}^{2}⁡x+x{csch}^{2}⁡x}{{coth}^{4}⁡x-{csch}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186537992 :question/problem "Solve for $v$ where $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{v+4}{{v}^{2}}-\\frac{7}{6v}=\\frac{5}{2{v}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n$v=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186538107 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=2 x y^{2} + 2 y z.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨e^{t},t + 3,e^{- t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$"] [17592186538122 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=x y^{2} + 3 x y z.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨e^{t},t + 1,e^{- t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$"] [17592186538128 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=- x y^{2} - 2 x y z.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨e^{t},t + 3,e^{- t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$"] [17592186538137 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=- 2 x^{2} z - 2 x \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\sin{\\left(t \\right)},3 t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2 \\pi],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$"] [17592186538139 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=- 2 x y^{2} + x y z.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨e^{t},t + 1,e^{- t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$"] [17592186538145 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=3 x y^{2} - 3 x y z.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨e^{t},t + 2,e^{- t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$"] [17592186538151 :question/problem "![](images/57/573013be1dc56a1ae4d0765b81b8e94006b8402a6da9706b118d43e286fb8291.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}6y\\,dx+9x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise."] [17592186538156 :question/problem "![](images/1e/1e97d150acbe82bf31d0a0e371122ae567d62279b66a5db2a3164180248a5e71.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-13dx+12x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise."] [17592186538161 :question/problem "![](images/24/245fb5eea54d303236a038f3d1b8253bc6c2e70426bcf0e5f48a40704b47d669.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}9y\\,dx+5x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise."] [17592186538171 :question/problem "![](images/d1/d1b2d83da42a1a95525ce3359e947800af884cd29f1ead6d9dbe4cee1c8dfb40.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}8y\\,dx-6x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise."] [17592186538181 :question/problem "![](images/48/483ce10aae9978fbc4c1b5a71173ab10b52bca0b15c84f01d7adb7247e82e436.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-2y\\,dx+3x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise."] [17592186538184 :question/problem "![](images/4a/4a1b8a23b4fb0d081f9d88748735e02b3a173843e5c4a1a40e70e6ba4e7335ad.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-6y\\,dx+8x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise."] [17592186538192 :question/problem "![](images/d2/d2039c49121a2da30efa96b7d456a043aa3860bc19399e67385c3f3268f6c0f8.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-13y\\,dx+12x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise."] [17592186538200 :question/problem "![](images/83/83357e8db109cd09ddcc51043caff588225edfd1b8f7b98dbb640a230ea7843d.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-13dx+6x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise."] [17592186538207 :question/problem "![](images/c9/c92f6dbb417fb4ff7bff5442d50d57777c74a9072c13bbc614f333f76e04d7f2.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-13y\\,dx+6x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise."] [17592186538926 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{(x+1)\\sqrt{9-(3x+3)^{2}}}.\n$$"] [17592186538936 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle ⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩\n$$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{3}-12xy+{y}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =24{x}^{2}-12y \\\\  & =12(2{x}^{2}-y) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8{x}^{3}-12xy+{y}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =-12x+3{y}^{2} \\\\  & =3({y}^{2}-4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨12(2{x}^{2}-y),\\,3({y}^{2}-4x),\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}12(2{x}^{2}-y)=0 \\\\ 3({y}^{2}-4x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=2{x}^{2}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({(2{x}^{2})}^{2}-4x) & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{4}-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x({x}^{3}-1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions of the last equation are $x=0,1.$ So, we have two cases.\n\n- For $x=0,$ we have \n\n$$\ny=2({0}^{2})=0.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=8({0}^{3})-12(0)(0)+{0}^{3}+1=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our first point is $(0,0,1).$\n\n- For $x=1,$ we have \n\n$$\ny=2({1}^{2})=2.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ and $y=2$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=8({1}^{3})-12(1)(2)+{2}^{3}+1=-7.\n$$\n\nSo, our second point is $(1,2,-7).$\n\nTherefore, the points where the tangent plane is horizontal are $(0,0,1)$ and $(1,2,-7).$"] [17592186538939 :question/problem "What is the sum of the series $$\n\\displaystyle \\pi -\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{5}}{5!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}{\\pi }^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯\\,?\n$$"] [17592186538945 :question/problem "What is the sum of the series $$\n\\displaystyle 1-\\frac{{(3\\pi /2)}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{(3\\pi /2)}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,(3\\pi /2)^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\,?\n$$\n\nThe sum of the series is\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186538946 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle 1-\\frac{25}{2!}+\\frac{625}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{5}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯=\n$$"] [17592186538952 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{6x-{x}^{2}-5}\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186538953 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{(6x-4)\\sqrt{9-(3x-2)^{2}}}.\n$$"] [17592186538960 :question/problem "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n$$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$38$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$34$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186538960 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2{y}_{1} & 2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 2+1 \\\\ 3+2\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 14 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{3}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & {x}_{1} & 1 \\\\ 1 & {x}_{3} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 14 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 38 & 52 & 34\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186538967 :question/problem "Consider the vector-valued function $f:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x},$ the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [17592186538967 :question/worked-solution "The total derivative of a vector-valued function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ with respect to a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{m\\times n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(x)$ where $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{3},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, the total derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{3}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{2}+{x}_{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the total derivative is a constant matrix, evaluating it at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$ gives the same matrix."] [17592186538968 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{(3x-15)\\sqrt{10x-{x}^{2}-24}}\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186538970 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int -\\frac{6}{|x+3|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x+13}}\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186538972 :question/problem "What is the sum of the series $$\n\\displaystyle \\,1-\\frac{1}{9\\cdot 2!}+\\frac{1}{81\\cdot 4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{3}^{2n}\\cdot (2n)!}+⋯\\,?\n$$\n\nThe sum of the series is\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186538975 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)\\sqrt{-4{x}^{2}-8x}}\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186538980 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{3}{|3x+1|\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}+6x+10}}\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186538982 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{12+4x-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186538986 :question/problem "What is the sum of the series $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{(5\\pi /6)^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{(5\\pi /6)^{5}}{5!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}(5\\pi /6)^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯\\,?\n$$"] [17592186538998 :question/problem "What is the sum of the series $$\n\\displaystyle \\,1-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{64\\cdot 2!}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{4}}{4\\,096\\cdot 4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{\\pi }^{2n}}{{8}^{2n}\\cdot (2n)!}+⋯\\,?\n$$"] [17592186539002 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\,1-\\frac{(\\pi /2)^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{(\\pi /2)^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{(\\pi /2)}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯=\n$$"] [17592186539007 :question/problem "What is the sum of the series $$\n\\displaystyle -3+\\frac{27}{3!}-\\frac{243}{5!}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}(-3)^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯\\,?\n$$"] [17592186539016 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{4-(x+3)^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186539017 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{12x-{x}^{2}-33}\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186539018 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{(x-2)\\sqrt{4x-{x}^{2}-3}}\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186539028 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{4}{|x+1|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2x+5}}\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186539029 :question/problem "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}^{3} \\\\ -{x}_{1}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}^{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n$$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$-3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186539029 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{3}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{2}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}(2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}^{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}(2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}^{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2{y}_{2} \\\\ 2 & 3{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}0-{1}^{3} \\\\ -1\\cdot 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{2}-{x}_{1}^{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}-{x}_{1}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(-{x}_{1}{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(-{x}_{1}{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}^{2} & 1 \\\\ -{x}_{2} & -{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186539122 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C 3 x y - 3 z^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle t^{2}, 2 t + 1, 3 t + 1\\rangle$ for $\\frac{1}{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{3}{2}$?"] [17592186539128 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C - 2 x y + 2 z^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle t^{2}, t - 3, 2 t + 1\\rangle$ for $1\\le t\\le \\frac{3}{2}$?"] [17592186539129 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C x y - z^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle 2 t^{2}, 2 t + 2, 3 t + 1\\rangle$ for $0\\le t\\le 2$?"] [17592186539157 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}2\\left(\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\right)\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j\\,$ for $0\\le t\\le 5?$"] [17592186539161 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\,+t\\,k$ for $\\,\\frac{\\pi}{12}\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}?$"] [17592186539171 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}2\\left(\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\right)\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j\\,$ for $2\\le t\\le \\frac{11}{2}?$"] [17592186539172 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}5\\left(z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\right)\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\,+t\\,k$ for $\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi}{6}?$"] [17592186539174 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}5\\left(\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\right)\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j\\,$ for $\\frac{1}{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{7}{3}?$"] [17592186539178 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C - 2 x y + 2 z^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle 3 t^{2}, t + 3, 2 t + 2\\rangle$ for $0\\le t\\le \\frac{5}{2}$?"] [17592186539200 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j\\,$ for $1\\le t\\le \\frac{4}{3}?$"] [17592186539202 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C2\\left((x^{3} - y)\\right)ds,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t) = 2ti + (2 - t)j$ for $- \\frac{3}{2}\\le t\\le 2$?"] [17592186539204 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C 3 x y - 3 z^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle 2 t^{2}, 2 t + 1, t + 3\\rangle$ for $1\\le t\\le \\frac{3}{2}$?"] [17592186540024 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=22-16=6\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{7}^{2}}{30}+\\frac{{12}^{2}}{32}}≈6.380\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 6\\pm 6.380\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186540028 :question/problem "Given the function \n$f(x;w)=2{w}_{1}{x}_{1}-5{w}_{2}{x}_{2},$\ncompute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$ with the input $$\n\\displaystyle x={[\\begin{aligned}-2,\\,8\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">${∇}_{w}\\,f=\\text{ }$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186540028 :question/worked-solution "To compute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to each component of $w.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{1}}(2{w}_{1}{x}_{1}-5{w}_{2}{x}_{2})=2{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{2}}(2{w}_{1}{x}_{1}-5{w}_{2}{x}_{2})=-5{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, with $$\n\\displaystyle x={[\\begin{aligned}-2,\\,8\\end{aligned}]}^{T},\n$$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}\\,f & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1} \\\\ -5{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -40\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186540042 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=18.8-18.5=0.3\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.960:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.960\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{1.6}^{2}}{20}+\\frac{{1.2}^{2}}{18}}≈0.894\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.3\\pm 0.894\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are $0.3\\pm 0.894.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The confidence interval contains $0:$\n\n$$\n(0.3-0.894,\\,0.3+0.894)=(-0.594,\\,1.194)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the confidence interval contains zero, it remains possible that ${\\mu }_{x}={\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186540048 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=(Ax-2b)^{T}(Ax-2b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [17592186540048 :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-2b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-2b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =2\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-2b) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}58 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186540051 :question/worked-solution "Notice that \n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=10-9=1\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.960:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.960\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{2}^{2}}{100}+\\frac{{1}^{2}}{150}}≈0.423\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 1\\pm 0.423\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are $1\\pm 0.423.$\n\n- Statement II is true. All values within the confidence interval are positive:\n\n$$\n(1-0.423,\\,1+0.423)=(0.577,\\,1.423)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since all values within the confidence interval are positive, this suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [17592186540057 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $$\n\\displaystyle x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186540057 :question/worked-solution "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0+2 \\\\ 0-4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186540059 :question/problem "Consider the two sets of $7$ paired observations shown in the table below.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $12$ | $14$ | $9$ | $17$ | $15$ | $10$ | $14$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $15$ | $18$ | $11$ | $10$ | $20$ | $15$ | $16$ |\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i}^{2}=1\\,231,\n$$ calculate ${S}_{xx}.$"] [17592186540059 :question/worked-solution "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of squared deviations from the mean for the data ${x}_{i}$ can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-\\frac{1}{n}{({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of the ${x}_{i}$ data:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i}=12+\\,14+\\,9+\\,17+\\,15+\\,10+\\,14=91.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i}^{2}=1\\,231.\n$$\n\nFinally, by substituting into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}=1\\,231-\\frac{1}{7}(91)^{2}=1231-1\\,183=48.\n$$"] [17592186540068 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $$\n\\displaystyle x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186540068 :question/worked-solution "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 0-10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186540069 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186540069 :question/worked-solution "Writing $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}Ax.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2Ax.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186540078 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (4-3{sech}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta =\n$$"] [17592186540099 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=19-11=8\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{5}^{2}}{32}+\\frac{{7}^{2}}{28}}≈3.118\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 8\\pm 3.118\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186540111 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=34-43=-9\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{3.4}^{2}}{58}+\\frac{{4.9}^{2}}{86}}≈1.356\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -9\\pm 1.356\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186540397 :question/problem "An aquarium initially holds $1200L$ of water containing $18kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.04kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $24L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $36L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{100}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{100})^{3}\n$$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186540415 :question/problem "An aquarium initially holds $1200L$ of water containing $25kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.05kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $24L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $36L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{100}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{100})^{3}\n$$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186540424 :question/problem "An aquarium initially holds $700L$ of water containing $20kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.05kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $20L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $30L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{70}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{70})^{3}\n$$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186540428 :question/problem "An aquarium initially holds $600L$ of water containing $29kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.07kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $24L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $36L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{50}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{50})^{3}\n$$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186540431 :question/problem "An aquarium initially holds $600L$ of water containing $13kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.05kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $24L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $36L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{50}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{50})^{3}\n$$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186541063 :question/problem "The eagle's total displacement in the $y$-direction over the first $6$ seconds is given by $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{6}{y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$ Calculate this displacement.\n\nThe eagle's total displacement in the $y$-direction over the first $6$ seconds is$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,\\text{m}.$"] [17592186541079 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}.$ \n\nNow, we need to find eigenvectors that correspond to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Computing $A-1I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are  orthogonal. So, we divide ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ by their respective norms to obtain the first and second columns of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186541080 :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded between the curves $y=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x$ and $$\n\\displaystyle y=-{x}^{3}+\\frac{7}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x.\n$$ Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186541080 :question/worked-solution "First, let $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x$ and $$\n\\displaystyle g(x)=-{x}^{3}+\\frac{7}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x.\n$$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x & =-{x}^{3}+\\frac{7}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+5x & =-2{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+5x \\\\ 2{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0$ and $x=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$\n\nNow, let's draw our usual vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](images/d1/d19b70836b7b377c498fa505868563115706b588b0a2216d09c6ec738f746bbd.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=f(x)=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $$\n\\displaystyle y=g(x)=-{x}^{3}+\\frac{7}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x([-{x}^{3}+\\frac{7}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x]-[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(-{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {(-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(-\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{{2}^{4}}{2})-0] \\\\  & =2\\pi (-\\frac{32}{5}+8) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{8}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186541091 :question/worked-solution "![](images/57/5778175613309c2b4b8007291ad122b879de1cd409ad3513f3f786c8f4885927.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{5}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nKnowing that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186541107 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first and third columns, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $$\n\\displaystyle [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{6}{7} \\\\ \\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}]\n$$ has both properties."] [17592186541124 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x(1)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"180px\">$3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-1))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-1))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\displaystyle \\,\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"180px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-{e}^{2s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186541124 :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-1)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-1)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-1))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-1))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-1)) \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-1))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-1))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2s} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -{e}^{2s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-1))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-1)) \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-1))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-1))\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{1}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -{e}^{2s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186541136 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-3.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}.$ \n\nNow, we need to find  eigenvectors that correspond to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are  orthogonal. So, we divide ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ by their respective norms, to obtain the first and second columns of $P:$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186541137 :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f0c1c9df42fc2ed772353baec0f81e796485d8bf3c1cd9dde234e74e39a04c56.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{2}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}$\n\nKnowing that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7\\cdot 2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186541140 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186541149 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x(1)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"190px\">$2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\displaystyle \\,\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"100px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-{e}^{s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186541149 :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{s} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -{e}^{s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1)\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{1}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -{e}^{s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186542118 :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{x}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$ for $0\\le x<4\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$"] [17592186542129 :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$ for $0\\le x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$"] [17592186542139 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=(4Ax-5b)^{T}(4Ax-5b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$832$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186542139 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=4Ax-5b\\in {R}^{2}.$ Then, $L(A)={y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y)=2y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}=2{y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=4Ax-5b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}=4{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-5{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=4{x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot 4{x}_{j} \\\\  & =8{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is $8$ times the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=8y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =8y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =8(4Ax-5b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =8(4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =8([\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -16\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}208 \\\\ -248\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 832 \\\\ 0 & -992\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186542146 :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{5\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-5 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-5 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(10)=1.471\\,127\\,674…≈1.471\\,128\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places. \n\nTo find the other solution in the given interval, we substract $\\pi $ from the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=1.471\\,128-\\pi ≈-1.670\\,465\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places. \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈(1.471\\,128)\\cdot (-1.670\\,465)≈-2.457\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [17592186542153 :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is inside the given domain, one solution is ${x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo find another solution in the domain, we subtract $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\pi =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$"] [17592186542157 :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{9\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 2\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+9 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-9 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{9}{2}\\right)=-1.352\\,127\\,380\\,…≈-1.352\\,127\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places. \n\nSince $-1.352\\,127$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\nTo generate the solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-1.352\\,127\\,380\\,…+\\pi =1.789\\,465\\,272\\,…≈1.789\\,465 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-1.352\\,127\\,380\\,…+2\\pi =4.931\\,057\\,926\\,…≈4.931\\,058\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈1.789\\,466\\cdot 4.931\\,058≈8.824\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [17592186542169 :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo find the other solution, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3}=-\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$"] [17592186542172 :question/worked-solution "We divide each term of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\sqrt{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo generate the solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$"] [17592186542179 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=\\frac{1}{2}(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186542179 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b\\in {R}^{2}.$ Then, $L(A)=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y)=y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}={y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}={x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =(Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186542183 :question/problem "Consider the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$ for $0\\le x<\\pi .$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are written in **ascending order**, what are their values? Express your answers in terms of $\\pi .$\n\n${x}_{1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,$\n${x}_{2}=${{field-2}}$,\\,$\n${x}_{3}=${{field-3}}$,\\,$\n${x}_{4}=${{field-4}}"] [17592186542189 :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the other solution in the given interval, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\n$$"] [17592186542190 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=2({y}^{T}Ax)$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 10 \\\\ -5 & -5\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [17592186542190 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=2(y\\cdot {x}^{T}).$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=2([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}9 & 0\\end{aligned}])=2([\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}18 & 0 \\\\ -18 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186542191 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186542192 :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$ for $0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$"] [17592186542195 :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ in **ascending order**, what are the values of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}?$ Express your answers in terms of $\\pi .$\n\n${x}_{1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,$\n${x}_{2}=${{field-2}}"] [17592186542200 :question/problem "Consider the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is ${x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$"] [17592186542202 :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the tangent line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta )={e}^{-\\theta }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-\\theta }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\cancel{{e}^{-\\theta }}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\cancel{{e}^{-\\theta }}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4} \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}).\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent:\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4} & =0\\cdot (x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4} & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/6b/6b8602110483fcc09d9ac0c04a16ab4e624f00a2ff752851e9c027bef5c9bc83.png)"] [17592186542212 :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{10\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =10 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5)≈1.373\\,40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places.\n\nTo find the other solution in the given interval, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\nx≈1.373\\,40+\\pi ≈4.514\\,99\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places. \n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to two decimal places, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}≈1.37,\\,{x}_{2}≈4.51.\n$$"] [17592186542213 :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-15\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{15\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{15\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 4\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-15 & =0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-15 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =15 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{15}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{15}{4}\\right)=75.068\\,582{…}^{∘}≈{75.069}^{∘}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nSince ${75.069}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={75.069}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={x}_{1}+{180}^{∘}=75.068\\,582{…}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}≈255.069\\,{…}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is $75.069\\cdot 255.069≈19\\,148$\nrounded to the nearest integer."] [17592186542219 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the relationship between ${\\theta }_{R}$ and $\\theta .$\n\n![](images/35/35e9a04cf71b42acdf7769ef541c5b93c9267130e965d52d3989f3a00692cea1.png)\n\nUsing ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{7},$ let's calculate all possible values of $\\theta .$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the first quadrant, then $\\theta ={\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{7}.$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the second quadrant, then $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{6\\pi }{7}.\n$$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the third quadrant, then $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{8\\pi }{7}.\n$$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the fourth quadrant, then $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R}=2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{13\\pi }{7}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta $ cannot be equal to $\\frac{5\\pi }{7}.$"] [17592186542220 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=8y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-8x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$*"] [17592186542220 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$ So, the matrix of the system is $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$"] [17592186542227 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186543471 :question/problem "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by {{field-1}} both sides of  the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines {{field-2}} by substitution.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ is obtained by substituting {{field-3}} into $\\text{L1}.$"] [17592186543473 :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the exact solutions to the equation $$\n\\displaystyle 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2}-{60}^{∘})+\\frac{3}{2}=0,\n$$ for $0\\le x<{360}^{∘},$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n {{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$"] [17592186543482 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Any $3\\times 4$ matrix $A$ has at most $4$ distinct singular values.\n\nII. Any $8\\times 6$ matrix $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values.\n\nIII. The matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ has one negative singular value."] [17592186543492 :question/problem "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by {{field-1}} both sides of  the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ into the {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from the fact that {{field-3}} matrix."] [17592186543493 :question/problem "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from the fact that {{field-1}} matrix.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by {{field-2}} both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ is obtained by substituting {{field-3}} into {{field-4}}."] [17592186543496 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=10$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}\\right)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{(x-\\theta )^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i}=41.2,\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i}^{2}=182.6,\n$$ and $n=10.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-\\frac{10}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(182.6-2\\theta \\cdot 41.2+10{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+(-91.3)+41.2\\theta +(-5){\\theta }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=(-91.3)+41.2+(-5)=-55.1.\n$$"] [17592186543504 :question/problem "Consider the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are written in ascending order,  what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{3}?$"] [17592186543509 :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the exact solutions to the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+{30}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$ for $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<{180}^{∘},$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$"] [17592186543511 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$ has one negative singular value.\n\nII. Any $2\\times 4$ matrix $A$ has at most $4$ distinct singular values.\n\nIII. Any $6\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $6$ distinct singular values."] [17592186543533 :question/problem "Consider the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{4}?$"] [17592186543548 :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$ for $0\\le x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$"]}
